Paediatric Asthma
Paediatric & Neonatal
Episodic expiratory wheezing, Nocturnal dry cough, Reversibility with bronchodilators

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-036
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of respiratory distress, audible wheezing, and unremitting cough severely disrupting sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage established a high risk of impending respiratory failure due to visible subcostal retractions and an inability to sleep due to continuous coughing. Immediate assessment was required to assess airway patency, evaluate oxygenation, and administer robust bronchodilator therapy prior to the next in-hours period to prevent hypoxic deterioration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They are coughing so hard they cannot sleep, and I can hear a loud whistling noise every time they breathe out."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with recurrent, distinctly episodic paroxysms heavily featuring diffuse expiratory wheezing directly accompanied by visible respiratory distress. The current episode began 48 hours ago following exposure to cold air. The symptoms have escalated tonight into a chronic, unremitting dry cough definitively worsening strictly at night, frequently interrupting deep sleep architecture. The patient exhibits noticeably decreased exercise tolerance today, severely resulting directly in an absolute reluctance to engage in strenuous active play. There is a known atopic diathesis, confirmed by a tight personal history of coexisting allergic rhinitis.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, fatigued from increased work of breathing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver anxiety regarding the rapid escalation of respiratory effort.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 125 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 32 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 22 Kg
General: Sitting upright, anxious, visibly utilizing accessory muscles to breathe.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals a prolonged expiratory phase noticeably altering the standard baseline ratio of normal breathing mechanics. Subcostal, intercostal, and suprasternal retractions objectively indicate profoundly increased inspiratory effort during this acute attack. Auscultation demonstrates widespread, loud expiratory wheezing. The chest is hyperinflated and distinctly barrel-shaped, reliably generating a diffuse hyperresonant percussion note. The diagnostic tetrad of episodic expiratory wheezing, nocturnal dry cough, and documented reversibility with bronchodilators is clearly established in the clinical history and current presentation.
Environmental Observations: Cold ambient room temperature; multiple plush toys on the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol inhaler administered twice by parents with only transient, mild effect due to poor inhaler technique without a spacer.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Paediatric Asthma - Status: Acute moderate exacerbation
Differentials:

Viral Bronchiolitis - Ruled out due to the patient's age (typically under 2 years for bronchiolitis) and the highly recurrent, episodic nature of the wheeze.

Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out by the bilateral, diffuse nature of the wheeze, gradual onset over 48 hours, and strict absence of a sudden, violent choking event.

Pneumonia - Ruled out by the absence of fever, strict lack of focal crepitations, and the prominent, diffuse expiratory wheezing.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of rapid respiratory fatigue)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of severe nocturnal wheezing, subcostal retractions, and borderline hypoxia necessitated an urgent after-hours clinical assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified an escalating respiratory crisis requiring immediate intervention to halt progression to severe bronchospasm and exhaustion. Objective clinical evaluation confirmed a moderate asthma exacerbation driven by environmental triggers, ruling out focal infection or foreign body aspiration. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a significant, unacceptable risk of total respiratory decompensation and hypoxic arrest. Immediate clinical intervention allowed for the supervised, correct administration of high-dose inhaled bronchodilators and systemic corticosteroids, rapidly stabilizing the airway in the community.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Salbutamol (6 puffs via spacer and mask) with explicit, rapid symptom improvement directly following the prompt administration. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 1 mg/kg stat.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict, visually tracked reward system for perfect inhaler technique. Provide a high-value token immediately after the child maintains a tight seal on the spacer mask for exactly 6 normal breaths per puff without pulling away. This directly reinforces the correct mechanical behavior necessary for the medication to reach the lower airways, transforming a terrifying medical intervention into a controlled, rewarding task.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of a dedicated pediatric spacer with mask.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 24-48 hours to assess response to oral corticosteroids and review the chronic asthma action plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes completely exhausted, unable to speak in full sentences, if the wheeze suddenly stops but breathing remains labored (silent chest), or if the lips turn blue.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Asthma is a dynamic, hyper-reactive airway disease; symptoms can re-escalate rapidly as the short-acting bronchodilator wears off.
Clinical Course: The oral steroids will take 4-6 hours to reduce the underlying inflammation. The child will require regular bronchodilator doses throughout the night.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the child's chest is sucking in deeply with every breath, or if they are too breathless to talk or drink.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The airways are severely inflamed and physically squeezing shut. The inhaler relaxes the muscle immediately, but the oral steroid is absolutely necessary to reduce the swelling over the next few days. Using the inhaler without the spacer means the medicine hits the back of the throat and never reaches the lungs.
Actionable Steps: Administer 6 puffs of Salbutamol via the spacer every 4 hours tonight. Wash all plush toys in hot water and remove them from the sleeping environment to reduce allergen load.

